<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Millicent Garrett Fawcett</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1847">1847</a>–<a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Millicent Fawcett</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Millicent Garrett Fawcett was an English political activist and writer who led the National Union of Women's Suffrage Societies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s largest women's rights association, from 1897 to 1919. She campaigned for the vote through changes in the law and distanced herself from the militancy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/womens-social-and-political-union">Women's Social and Political Union</a>. She co-founded what became Newnham College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, in 1871, and in 1901 led a commission of women sent to investigate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a> of the South African War. In 2018 she became the first woman honoured with a statue in Parliament Square, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/millicent-garrett-fawcett.jpg" alt="Millicent Garrett Fawcett" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam Cuerden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Millicent_Fawcett.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
